╭─ STRIX ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Penetration test summary                                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  # Executive Summary                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  An external penetration test of the OWASP Juice Shop web application        │
│  (http://juiceshop:3000) identified multiple critical security weaknesses    │
│  that allow full compromise of confidentiality and integrity with minimal    │
│  attacker effort. The most severe issues enable administrative access        │
│  without legitimate credentials, including SQL injection in the login        │
│  endpoint, a JWT signature verification bypass (accepting unsigned           │
│  “alg=none” tokens), and a mass-assignment flaw that allows                  │
│  self-registration as an administrator.\n\nIn addition, the application      │
│  exposes sensitive files through an unauthenticated directory listing        │
│  (including a downloadable KeePass database), contains a critical DOM-based  │
│  cross-site scripting vulnerability capable of stealing authentication       │
│  tokens, and exhibits multiple broken access control/IDOR conditions         │
│  enabling cross-user basket and address manipulation. These issues           │
│  collectively indicate systemic gaps in input handling, token validation,    │
│  and authorization enforcement.\n\nOverall risk posture: Critical.           │
│  Immediate remediation is required to prevent account takeover, privileged   │
│  actions by unauthenticated users, sensitive data exfiltration, and          │
│  fraudulent business operations.                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  # Methodology                                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  Testing was performed as an authorized external, black-box web application  │
│  assessment aligned with the OWASP Web Security Testing Guide (WSTG) and     │
│  common penetration testing practices.\n\nActivities included                │
│  attack-surface discovery (endpoint mapping, parameter identification,       │
│  JavaScript bundle analysis, and API enumeration), authentication and        │
│  session testing (registration, login, password reset, 2FA workflows, token  │
│  handling), authorization testing (object-level and function-level access    │
│  control across REST and API namespaces), injection testing (SQL injection   │
│  validation and controlled extraction), client-side security testing (DOM    │
│  XSS), file handling assessment (exposed file repositories and upload        │
│  workflows), and security misconfiguration review (error handling,           │
│  diagnostics exposure, and security header behavior).\n\nOnly findings with  │
│  reproducible security impact were treated as confirmed vulnerabilities and  │
│  documented with proof-of-concept evidence in individual vulnerability       │
│  reports.                                                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  # Technical Analysis                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity model\nSeverity reflects practical exploitability and business     │
│  impact across confidentiality, integrity, and availability.\n\nConfirmed    │
│  findings overview\nMultiple independent paths to administrative compromise  │
│  were confirmed.\n\nCritical: SQL injection leading to authentication        │
│  bypass\nSQL injection in the login workflow enables bypassing credential    │
│  checks and obtaining an administrator session/token. This provides direct   │
│  access to protected administrative APIs.\n\nCritical: JWT signature         │
│  verification bypass (acceptance of unsigned tokens)\nThe backend accepts    │
│  unsigned JWTs using “alg=none”, enabling attackers to forge arbitrary       │
│  identities and roles. Validation also affects step-up authentication in     │
│  the 2FA verification flow, expanding the attack surface of the              │
│  weakness.\n\nCritical: Mass assignment in registration enables              │
│  self-registration as administrator\nThe unauthenticated user creation       │
│  endpoint accepts a client-supplied role and persists it, allowing           │
│  attackers to create durable administrator accounts without prior            │
│  privileges.\n\nCritical: DOM-based XSS in client-side search route\nThe     │
│  application’s client-side search route renders attacker-controlled input    │
│  from the URL fragment as HTML, enabling arbitrary JavaScript execution.     │
│  Authentication tokens were confirmed to be accessible to injected script    │
│  from browser storage, enabling account takeover.\n\nCritical/High: SQL      │
│  injection in product search\nThe product search endpoint contains           │
│  exploitable SQL injection (SQLite), enabling database metadata extraction   │
│  and broader data access.\n\nHigh: Sensitive file exposure via               │
│  unauthenticated directory listing\nAn unauthenticated file repository       │
│  exposes internal documents and downloadable artifacts, including a KeePass  │
│  database file. This represents a direct pathway to credential compromise    │
│  and subsequent lateral movement.\n\nHigh: Broken access control             │
│  (IDOR/BOLA) across customer objects\nMultiple object-level authorization    │
│  failures were confirmed, including cross-user basket read and basket item   │
│  modification, and address ownership takeover via update operations. These   │
│  enable cross-user data access and integrity compromise.\n\nHigh:            │
│  Authorization and token scoping failures across APIs\nA standard customer   │
│  token was confirmed to access endpoints that should be restricted           │
│  (including user directory enumeration) and to invoke B2B order creation     │
│  workflows, indicating missing role enforcement and inadequate               │
│  audience/issuer/scoping controls.\n\nHigh: Open redirect with allowlist     │
│  bypass\nThe redirector endpoint can be abused to redirect users to          │
│  attacker-controlled destinations via allowlist bypass. This materially      │
│  increases phishing and token theft risk, especially when chained with       │
│  other weaknesses.\n\nMedium: Verbose error handling reveals internal        │
│  implementation details\nError responses disclose stack traces and internal  │
│  paths, increasing attacker efficiency for targeted exploitation and         │
│  reconnaissance.\n\nMedium: Unauthenticated Prometheus metrics               │
│  exposure\nThe metrics endpoint discloses detailed operational telemetry     │
│  (runtime versions, process stats, and application counters), increasing     │
│  reconnaissance capability and facilitating targeted                         │
│  exploitation.\n\nSystemic themes\nThe application exhibits recurring        │
│  patterns of missing or inconsistent authorization checks, unsafe trust in   │
│  client-controlled data (tokens and roles), and insufficient input/output    │
│  encoding on client-side rendering paths. These themes create multiple       │
│  independent and chainable attack paths.                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  # Recommendations                                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  Immediate priority\nRemediate authentication-bypass and                     │
│  privilege-escalation conditions. Fix the login SQL injection and the        │
│  product search SQL injection using parameterized queries and strict input   │
│  handling. Correct JWT verification to reject “alg=none” and enforce strict  │
│  signature validation and claims validation (issuer, audience, expiration)   │
│  consistently across all token usage, including 2FA temporary tokens.        │
│  Prevent role elevation by implementing strict server-side allowlisting of   │
│  writable fields on registration and update endpoints and overriding any     │
│  client-supplied privilege fields.\n\nRemove direct sensitive data           │
│  exposure. Disable unauthenticated directory listing and remove sensitive    │
│  artifacts from web-accessible paths. Treat the exposed credential vault as  │
│  compromised and rotate any credentials it may contain. Restrict access to   │
│  operational endpoints such as metrics to trusted networks or authenticated  │
│  administrative contexts.\n\nShort-term priority\nCentralize authorization   │
│  enforcement. Implement deny-by-default authorization middleware for all     │
│  API namespaces, and enforce object ownership checks for all reads and       │
│  writes (basket, basket items, addresses, users). Add automated negative     │
│  authorization tests to prevent regressions.\n\nFix DOM XSS by ensuring      │
│  client-side routes render untrusted input as text, not HTML, and apply      │
│  robust encoding/sanitization. Implement a strict Content Security Policy    │
│  that prevents inline script execution and event-handler injection. Reduce   │
│  token exposure by avoiding storing bearer tokens in JavaScript-accessible   │
│  storage and using defense-in-depth cookie attributes where                  │
│  applicable.\n\nMedium-term priority\nHarden redirect handling by            │
│  restricting redirects to relative paths or enforcing strict URL parsing     │
│  and exact allowlists on scheme/host/port; explicitly reject script          │
│  schemes. Improve error handling to suppress stack traces and internal       │
│  paths in user-facing responses while retaining server-side                  │
│  logging.\n\nRetest guidance\nAfter remediation, conduct a focused retest    │
│  of token validation, authentication flows (including 2FA), access control   │
│  for all object types, and client-side route handling for DOM XSS. Perform   │
│  regression testing for SQL injection across all search and authentication   │
│  code paths and validate that sensitive endpoints and files are no longer    │
│  accessible to unauthenticated users.                                        │
│                                                                              │
│                                                                              │
╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯

